---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Store Brands",
    tags: [
      "equate protein powder",
      "equate whey protein powder",
      "equate plant based protein powder",
      "walmart equate protein",
      "is equate protein powder good"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Walmart Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-walmart-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Equate Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What Walmart sells under its Equate label, what is on each tub, what it costs per 25 g of protein, what independent lead testing found, and which name brands to buy instead when you shop on Amazon.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-equate-sells">What Equate Sells</a>
      <a href="#equate-whey">Equate Whey Label</a>
      <a href="#equate-plant">Equate Plant Label</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#third-party">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-name-brands">Equate vs Gold Standard</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Equate protein powder is Walmart's store brand, sold as a whey with 24 g of protein per 31 g scoop and a pea, rice and quinoa plant powder with 21 g per 40 g serving, per the labels at the time of writing. The whey is one of the cheapest proteins at Walmart, at about $0.94 per 25 g. Neither tub carries a third-party certification. Below: both labels, cost, a Consumer Reports lead result and Amazon swaps.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Equate Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equate protein powder is worth buying if you shop at Walmart and want cheap, lean whey: the 24 oz Equate whey gives 24 g of protein in a 120-calorie scoop for about $0.94 per 25 g of protein, per the label and the Walmart price we saw. That is cheaper per gram than almost every name brand on the same shelf, and the label reads almost exactly like Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Equate plant-based powder is a weaker buy. It gives 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving, but at about $1.54 per 25 g and 620 mg of sodium per serving it costs more than the whey and carries more salt than most plant tubs. Neither Equate powder publishes third-party testing, so <strong>buy it if</strong> you are a recreational lifter or just want more protein in your day, and <strong>skip it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete or want a certified label.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Equate product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Equate Whey, 24 oz (22 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g (31 g scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.87</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.94</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Equate Plant-Based, Rich Chocolate, 14.1 oz (10 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g (40 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12.94</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.54</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Equate Collagen Peptides, 10 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$16.94</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not a complete protein</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures are per the labels and Walmart's listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor and store. Equate is a Walmart-only brand, so you will not find it on Amazon; the cards below pair each Equate tub with the closest Amazon alternative. For the full Walmart aisle ranked by cost per gram, see our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">best Walmart protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Equate Protein Powder and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first two cards are Equate's own whey and plant-based powders. Equate is a Walmart-only brand: it is sold in Walmart stores and on Walmart.com, not on Amazon, so those cards carry no Amazon link of their own. The other three are the closest Amazon alternatives we could verify, with the live Amazon price we saw and the cost per 25 g of protein worked out from the label.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-equate-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Protein Powders Does Equate Sell at Walmart?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walmart currently lists two Equate protein powders, a whey and a plant-based blend, plus an Equate collagen peptides powder that is often shelved beside them. Equate is Walmart's private label for health and personal care, the same name you see on pain relievers, vitamins and first-aid kits. The protein line is small next to the name brands it sits with, but it covers the two choices most shoppers are making: dairy whey or dairy-free plant protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Equate Whey Protein Powder</strong> comes in a 24 oz tub of 22 servings in Smooth Vanilla and Rich Chocolate. Walmart's listing describes it as 24 g of protein and 5.5 g of BCAAs per serving, with whey protein isolate as the first ingredient. It is the product most people mean when they search for Equate protein powder, and the one with by far the most Walmart reviews.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Equate Plant-Based Protein Powder</strong> is sold in Rich Chocolate (14.1 oz) and Smooth Vanilla (13.4 oz), both listed at $12.94 when we checked, and Walmart also shows a larger 2 lb Rich Chocolate tub. Listings for the 2 lb size quote different protein figures from the small tub, so read the panel on the container you pick up rather than trusting the product title. The <strong>Equate Grass Fed Hydrolyzed Bovine Collagen Peptides</strong> come unflavored in 10 oz and 20 oz sizes, with 9 g of protein per serving per Walmart's listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also find older Equate whey listings, including a larger tub with 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving and maltodextrin in the ingredients. Those appear to be an earlier formula. And Equate sells ready-to-drink protein shakes in 11 fl oz bottles, which turn up in the same search results but are not powder. We cover them briefly in the cost section below, and our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide ranks shakes properly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT TO CHECK ON THE TUB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Equate labels change between sizes and formulas.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Old and new Equate whey tubs list different scoop sizes, protein and ingredients. Before you compare prices, confirm the serving size, grams of protein and servings per container on the supplement-facts panel of the exact tub on the shelf.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="equate-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Equate Whey Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equate whey protein powder is a blend of whey protein isolate and whey protein concentrate, flavored, thickened with carrageenan and sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Per the Smooth Vanilla label at the time of writing, one 31 g scoop provides 24 g of protein, about 120 calories, 1.5 g of fat (0.5 g saturated), 3 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of total sugar with no added sugar, 35 mg of cholesterol, 130 mg of sodium and 140 mg of calcium. Rich Chocolate figures differ slightly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The full ingredient list is short: protein blend (whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate), natural flavors, sunflower lecithin, salt, carrageenan, sucralose and acesulfame potassium. It is labeled as containing milk. There is no soy lecithin, no maltodextrin filler and no added creatine or vitamins, which makes it a plain everyday whey rather than a mass gainer or meal replacement.
          </p>

          <p>
            The number that matters most is protein density. Equate gets 24 g of protein from 31 g of powder, about 77% protein by weight. Budget blends such as Six Star and Body Fortress give 30 g of protein from scoops of roughly 45 g or more, which is a lower share of protein and more calories per serving. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard gives the same 24 g per 31 g scoop as Equate, which is why the two are compared so often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate first does not mean pure isolate. Because the label lists a blend, it contains some concentrate, which carries a little more lactose and fat than isolate alone. Most people with mild lactose sensitivity handle a blend like this fine in a single scoop, but if whey concentrate gives you trouble, a pure isolate is the safer bet; our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            The additives are the main reason some shoppers pass. Sucralose and acesulfame potassium are FDA-approved sweeteners, but people avoiding artificial sweeteners will want a different tub; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide lists options. Carrageenan is an approved thickener that some people prefer to avoid. Anyone with a milk allergy, kidney disease or a medical condition that limits protein should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="equate-plant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Equate Plant-Based Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Equate plant-based protein powder is a pea, rice and quinoa blend with added BCAAs, sweetened with erythritol and sucralose. Per the Rich Chocolate label at the time of writing, a two-scoop, 40 g serving gives 21 g of protein, about 150 calories, 3.5 g of fat, 11 g of carbohydrate including 3 g of fiber, 0 g of sugar and 620 mg of sodium. The tub holds 10 servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is longer than the whey's: a protein blend of pea protein, rice protein and quinoa seed powder; erythritol; alkalized cocoa; natural flavors; a sunflower oil creamer made with maltodextrin, modified food starch and mono- and diglycerides; a gum blend of gum arabic, cellulose gum, xanthan gum and carrageenan; a BCAA blend of leucine, isoleucine and valine; sea salt; and sucralose. Walmart's listing says it supplies 3.9 g of BCAAs per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing pea with rice is a sensible formula choice, because the two proteins cover each other's weaker amino acids, and the added leucine nudges the blend closer to whey for muscle building. The creamer and gums are there to fix the gritty texture plant proteins are known for. What stands out on the panel is the sodium: 620 mg per serving is about a quarter of the 2,300 mg daily value and roughly double what many plant powders carry. If you watch your salt intake, that matters more than the protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Erythritol is a sugar alcohol that most people tolerate, though large amounts can cause bloating; combined with gums, it is a common reason buyers report stomach upset from plant powders. The 21 g of protein is fine, but it costs 40 g of powder and 150 calories to get there, compared with 31 g and 120 calories for Equate's whey. For a wider look at dairy-free tubs, see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> ranking and our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> explainer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins also tend to carry more heavy metals than whey, because rice and pea plants take up lead and cadmium from soil. We found no published heavy-metal result for the Equate plant powder. That does not mean it is high, only that nobody has published a number. If you choose a plant powder for daily use, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide lists tubs with published test results.
          </p>
        </div>

