        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Sticker Prices Instead of Protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The $12.94 Equate plant tub looks cheaper than the $19.87 whey, but it holds 210 g of protein against the whey's 528 g. Per 25 g of protein, the plant tub costs about $1.54 and the whey about $0.94. Always divide the price by total grams of protein before you decide which tub is the bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Store Brand Means Tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Walmart label does not mean the powder is certified. Equate lists no NSF, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project program, and Walmart does not name the manufacturer. Consumer Reports' low lead result for the chocolate whey is encouraging, but it is a one-time snapshot, not ongoing lot testing or banned-substance screening.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Product Title Over the Panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Equate listings mix old and new formulas. One older whey tub lists 30 g of protein per two-scoop serving with maltodextrin, and 2 lb plant listings quote different protein figures from the small tub. Read the supplement-facts panel on the container in your hand, not the headline on the shelf tag or website.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Plant Powder's Sodium</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Equate's plant-based powder has 620 mg of sodium per serving, about a quarter of the daily value. Two shakes a day would add roughly 1,240 mg before any food. If you have high blood pressure or follow a low-sodium diet, check the panel and consider Orgain's 390 mg or another lower-sodium plant tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Equate Collagen for Muscle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Equate's collagen peptides give 9 g of protein per serving, but collagen has no tryptophan and little leucine, so it does not support muscle the way whey does. It fills a different role. If building or keeping muscle is the goal, spend the money on Equate whey, which costs less per gram anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

